body: 'turntable_stator' | material: PET Plastic | appearance: Plastic - Glossy (White)
DATUM: z 0 is the printed platter rim top (its z 42.00, MEASURED)
base plate r 10.0..50.0 x 4.0, 3 posts r 45.0 dia 11.0 to z 29.50
rollers: 3 x 608ZZ at r 27.0, az (90.0, 210.0, 330.0), axles RADIAL at z 13.10
  stub 7.90 dia from r 35.00 to 22.50 past a 11.0 collar 0.50 proud
  cheeks r 31.00..35.00, top z 19.10 (rotor rides at 24.10)
RACE FACE z 24.10 = axle 13.10 + 608 OD/2. Rotor rim top 29.10, ring under 29.50.
J1 SPLINE DRIVE - NO HORN. deck 10.20, case top 16.90, boss top 21.10, spline top 23.60
  the rotor race face is at 24.10, so the spline stops 0.50 SHORT of it - nothing on the servo touches the rotor and the rollers carry the arm
  hub: O4.65 broach over 2.50 of spline, 2.10 left with the ring fully lifted (min 2.00). Bolted to the rotor, 3 x M3 on r 8.00.
  hub body z 17.30..24.10 - built by NEITHER of J1's two host parts. specs/robotarm/j1_hub.md.
SG90: tabs bear at z 10.20, case bottom z -6.80 (into the platter column)
  bearing seat: 11.0 collar on a 12.0 inner race, retained by a 11.0 washer (NOT a standard M3 Ø7 - it falls through the Ø8 bore)
  SHAFT ON AXIS: case centre offset +5.25 in Y so the output shaft
    lands at y 0. The SG90 shaft is 6.1 from the near end of a 22.7
    case, so centring the CASE would put it 5.2 mm off the J1 axis.
  ONE pilot 1.8 at y +19.1 (FAR tab). The near tab at y -8.7 has no screw:
    the lead keyway runs straight through it and lead_y = 0 means it cannot
    be moved aside. ONE screw and a 0.5-per-side pocket is all that holds
    this servo against its own reaction torque - see the keyway note: a
    bridge cap over the near tab is the outstanding fix.
  LEAD KEYWAY 3.6 wide, centred, END wall at y -6.1, out to y -12.0
  pad 18 x 33 centred y +5.25
CABLE WINDOW: r 13.7..20.0, 70 deg at az 270 - the fixed half of the
  loom route. The servo well is 12.40 x 22.90 on a 12.20 x 22.70 case,
  so NOTHING could pass beside the servo - 15 leads cross J1 here.
  base_lid repeats it below; shoulder_bracket's window is the moving half.
ONE M3 per post: ring -> post -> platter insert. 3 screws hold the turret.
